Q: Spring Boot → Quarkus annotation / concept cheat sheet

Memorize this table — interview gold for Spring developers.

────────────────────────────────────────
Bootstrap / DI

| Spring Boot                      | Quarkus                                      |
| -------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `@SpringBootApplication`         | (no equivalent bootstrap annotation)         |
| `@Component` / `@Service`        | `@ApplicationScoped` (or other CDI scope)    |
| `@Repository`                    | `@ApplicationScoped` + Panache/repo          |
| `@Autowired`                     | `@Inject`                                    |
| Constructor injection            | Constructor `@Inject` (preferred)            |
| `@Value("${x}")`                 | `@ConfigProperty(name="x")`                  |
| `@ConfigurationProperties`       | `@ConfigMapping`                             |
| `@Configuration` + `@Bean`       | CDI producer (`@Produces`) / build items     |

────────────────────────────────────────
REST

| Spring Boot                         | Quarkus                         |
| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| `@RestController`                   | `@Path` resource class          |
| `@RequestMapping` / `@GetMapping`   | `@GET` `@POST` `@PUT` `@DELETE` |
| `@PathVariable`                     | `@PathParam`                    |
| `@RequestParam`                     | `@QueryParam`                   |
| `@RequestBody`                      | method arg (JSON provider)      |
| `ResponseEntity<>`                  | `Response` / entity return      |
| Spring `RestTemplate`/`WebClient`   | `@RegisterRestClient` interface |

────────────────────────────────────────
Data / transactions

| Spring Boot                 | Quarkus                                      |
| --------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Spring Data `JpaRepository` | PanacheRepository **or** PanacheEntity       |
| `@Entity`                   | `@Entity` (same JPA idea)                    |
| `@Transactional` (Spring)   | `@Transactional` (**jakarta.transaction**)   |
| `Pageable` / `Page`         | Panache `Page` / manual query + range        |
| Flyway/Liquibase            | Quarkus Flyway/Liquibase extensions          |

────────────────────────────────────────
Cross-cutting

| Spring Boot        | Quarkus                                      |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| `@Scheduled`       | `@Scheduled` (Quarkus scheduler; attrs differ) |
| `@Cacheable`       | `@CacheResult` / `@CacheInvalidate`          |
| `@Async`           | reactive/`@Blocking` patterns / buses        |
| Actuator           | SmallRye Health/Metrics under `/q/*`         |
| Spring Security    | Quarkus Security / OIDC / SmallRye JWT       |

────────────────────────────────────────
Reactive

| Spring            | Quarkus        |
| ----------------- | -------------- |
| `Mono<T>`         | `Uni<T>`       |
| `Flux<T>`         | `Multi<T>`     |
| Project Reactor   | Mutiny         |

────────────────────────────────────────
Config files / profiles (Spring Boot vs Quarkus)

Both use `application.properties` / yaml.
Difference is mostly **how profiles are written**.

Goal example:
- DEV  → H2 in-memory
- PROD → PostgreSQL

────────────────────────────────────────
A) Spring Boot implementation

Option 1 — separate files (most common)

application.properties
spring.application.name=my-app
# optional default profile
# spring.profiles.active=dev

application-dev.properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:dev
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update

application-prod.properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://db-host:5432/appdb
spring.datasource.username=app
spring.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate

Activate:
- IDE/env: SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=dev
- run: java -jar app.jar --spring.profiles.active=prod
- or in application.properties: spring.profiles.active=dev

Option 2 — single YAML with documents

application.yml
spring:
  application:
    name: my-app
---
spring:
  config:
    activate:
      on-profile: dev
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:h2:mem:dev
    username: sa
    password:
---
spring:
  config:
    activate:
      on-profile: prod
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://db-host:5432/appdb
    username: app
    password: ${DB_PASSWORD}

────────────────────────────────────────
B) Quarkus implementation

Quarkus prefers **one file** with `%profile.` prefixes
(also supports application-dev.properties style, but `%dev.` is idiomatic).

application.properties
quarkus.application.name=my-app

# DEV
%dev.quarkus.datasource.db-kind=h2
%dev.quarkus.datasource.jdbc.url=jdbc:h2:mem:dev
%dev.quarkus.datasource.username=sa
%dev.quarkus.datasource.password=
%dev.quarkus.hibernate-orm.database.generation=drop-and-create

# PROD
%prod.quarkus.datasource.db-kind=postgresql
%prod.quarkus.datasource.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://db-host:5432/appdb
%prod.quarkus.datasource.username=app
%prod.quarkus.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}
%prod.quarkus.hibernate-orm.database.generation=none

Activate:
- DEV is often automatic with `quarkus:dev`
- PROD: quarkus.profile=prod
  or env: QUARKUS_PROFILE=prod

────────────────────────────────────────
Side-by-side mental map

| Need              | Spring Boot                         | Quarkus                          |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Dev DB URL        | application-dev.properties          | %dev.quarkus.datasource.jdbc.url |
| Prod DB URL       | application-prod.properties         | %prod.quarkus.datasource.jdbc.url|
| Activate profile  | spring.profiles.active=prod         | quarkus.profile=prod             |
| Env var style     | SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE              | QUARKUS_PROFILE                  |
| Inject one value  | @Value / @ConfigurationProperties   | @ConfigProperty / @ConfigMapping |

Same idea: different config per environment.
Different syntax: Spring file-per-profile vs Quarkus `%profile.` keys.

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

“Same layered architecture; different APIs — Autowired→Inject, RestController→Path/GET, JpaRepository→Panache,
and build-time wiring instead of Spring runtime auto-config.”
